course: dictionary
lesson: 4833
title: A hut is a simple home
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A hut is a small, simple building. People have built huts out of wood, mud, straw and metal, to shelter in or to store things. You can often work out the meaning from the words around it: «Ice Age people hunted mammoths and used every part; they built huts out of mammoth bones.» Built tells you a hut is made, and out of tells you what it is made from.
explain: Look at the places in the words too. «A beach hut», «a garden hut» and «a ski hut» are all small buildings used for a purpose: a beach hut for changing or sitting, a garden hut for tools, a ski hut for shelter. The word that comes before hut often tells you what it is for.
explain: Stories use hut in a special way. A hut in a story is often a poor or lonely home, like a woodcutter's hut in the forest. Ask yourself: what would fit in this sentence instead of hut? If «a small, simple house» still makes sense, you have probably found the right meaning.
fact: A hut is a small, simple building.
fact: Huts can be made of wood, mud, straw or metal.
fact: Huts are used for shelter or for storage.
fact: Stone Age people mostly lived in huts, tents and camps in the open, and caves were used for shelter at times.
fact: About 15,000 years ago, at Mezhyrich in Ukraine, people built round huts from mammoth jaws, skulls, tusks and long bones.
exercise: What is a hut? | A tall tower made of glass | *A small, simple building used for shelter or storage | A kind of boat for fishing | A large palace with many rooms
exercise: «Bathing machines were wooden huts on wheels that were pulled into the sea.» What were the huts made of? | Glass | *Wood | Ice | Stone
exercise: «Scott's and Shackleton's huts still stand after over 100 years.» Find and copy the word that means still standing, not fallen down. | *stand | Scott's | huts | years
test: «At Mezhyrich in Ukraine, people built round huts from mammoth bones.» What were the huts built from? | Mud and straw | *Mammoth jaws, skulls, tusks and long bones | Glass and steel | Blocks of ice
test: Why were bathing machines pulled into the sea? | *So people could change inside and step straight into the water without being seen | So the huts could sail away | To keep the huts warm | To cook fish for lunch
exam: Which description fits a hut best? | A type of bread baked in an oven | *A small, simple building used for shelter or storage | A huge stadium for football matches | A kind of fish with long, thin fins
